<DocsLayout title="Knowledge Graph" description="Explicitly link entities to build a queryable relationship network using Engrams CLI.">
  <h1>Knowledge Graph &amp; Relationships</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Capture how decisions relate to patterns, how tasks track decisions, and how configurations depend on other entities. Build a queryable relationship network that gives your AI agent complete workspace topology.
  </p>

  <h2>Creating Links</h2>
  <p>
    Use the <code>engrams link add</code> command to connect any two entities in your database:
  </p>
  <pre><code>engrams link add \
  --source-type system_pattern --source-id 2 \
  --target-type decision --target-id 7 \
  --rel implements \
  --description "JWT token verification middleware"</code></pre>
  <p>
    Canonical relationships (see the ontology below) are validated against shared algebraic rules. Pass <code>--force</code> to record an intentional override when a link violates one.
  </p>

  <h2>Supported Entity Types</h2>
  <p>
    You can establish links between the following core entity types in the database:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>decision</code> — Architectural or design decisions (ADRs).</li>
    <li><code>system_pattern</code> — Codebase patterns or design standards.</li>
    <li><code>progress_entry</code> — Task tracking and status progress.</li>
    <li><code>custom_data</code> — Configuration key-value data.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Additionally, you can link decisions and patterns to external **Pull Request (PR)** URLs. The interactive HTML dashboard renders PR links as distinct visual nodes (under the relationship type <code>implemented_in</code>), allowing you to trace design decisions directly back to the pull request discussion thread.
  </p>
  <h2>Relationship Ontology</h2>
  <p>
    v0.8.0 introduces a canonical relationship vocabulary with shared semantics. Each canonical relationship declares a direction, transitivity, an inverse (auto-materialized on <code>graph rebuild</code>), and disjointness rules. Asserting a canonical relationship validates it against these rules and rejects violations — a <code>supersedes</code> link across mismatched entity types, or a <code>depends_on</code> sitting alongside a <code>conflicts_with</code> on the same pair — unless you pass <code>--force</code>. Free-form relationship names still pass through untouched and are surfaced by <code>engrams doctor</code>.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Relationship</th><th>Direction</th><th>Transitive</th><th>Inverse</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>relates_to</code></td><td>Symmetric</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>General association; any entity types</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>depends_on</code></td><td>Directed</td><td>Yes</td><td><code>depended_on_by</code></td><td>Disjoint with <code>conflicts_with</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>part_of</code></td><td>Directed</td><td>Yes</td><td><code>contains</code></td><td>Containment hierarchy</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>implements</code></td><td>Directed</td><td>—</td><td><code>implemented_by</code></td><td>A pattern or task realizes a decision</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>refines</code></td><td>Directed</td><td>Yes</td><td><code>refined_by</code></td><td>Same-type only; disjoint with <code>supersedes</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>supersedes</code></td><td>Directed</td><td>Yes</td><td><code>superseded_by</code></td><td>Replaces an older decision; one successor allowed</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>conflicts_with</code></td><td>Symmetric</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>Same-type only; disjoint with <code>depends_on</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>anchored_to</code></td><td>Directed</td><td>—</td><td><code>anchors</code></td><td>Links an entity to a file node</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>implemented_in</code></td><td>Directed</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>Links to a PR or commit node</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The <code>co_changes</code> relationship is derived automatically from git history (via <code>graph ingest</code>) rather than asserted by hand.
  </p>

  <h2>Listing and Querying Links</h2>
  <p>
    Retrieve all linked items for a specific entity using the <code>list</code> subcommand:
  </p>
  <pre><code>engrams link list --item-type decision --item-id 7</code></pre>
  <p>
    You can filter the query to only return specific relationship types or linked entity types:
  </p>
  <pre><code># Find all patterns that implement decision 7
engrams link list --item-type decision --item-id 7 --rel implements --linked-type system_pattern</code></pre>

  <h2>Transitive Traversal &amp; Inverse Edges</h2>
  <p>
    Because <code>depends_on</code>, <code>supersedes</code>, <code>part_of</code>, and <code>refines</code> are transitive, engrams can walk their closures to answer reachability questions — for example, "what transitively breaks if I revisit this decision?". Use <code>graph chain</code> from any node:
  </p>
  <pre><code># Everything that transitively depends on decision 7
engrams graph chain --item-type decision --item-id 7 --rel depends_on

# Full supersession chain starting at decision 3
engrams graph chain --node decision:3 --rel supersedes</code></pre>
  <p>
    Inverse relationships (<code>superseded_by</code>, <code>depended_on_by</code>, <code>contains</code>, <code>implemented_by</code>, <code>refined_by</code>, <code>anchors</code>) are materialized automatically by <code>graph rebuild</code>, so consumers never need to know which direction a link was originally asserted in. <code>engrams doctor</code> warns if any transitive relation forms a cycle.
  </p>

  <h2>Practical Benefit</h2>
  <p>
    By building these relations, your AI assistant can recursively query the graph to understand why certain code files are written the way they are. For example, if it sees a pattern in place, it can retrieve the linked decision to understand the business logic and constraints behind it.
  </p>
